WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.264 --> 00:00:02.250
- [Sal] In other videos,
we touched on the notion

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:06.530
of kinetic molecular theory,
which I'll just shorten as KMT.

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:10.220
And it's just this idea that
if you imagine a container,

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:11.950
I'll just draw it in 2-dimensions here,

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:15.820
that it contains some gas,
you can imagine the gas

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:20.500
as being these particles
where their collective volume

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.830
is much smaller than the
volume of the container.

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:25.180
And the temperature we're dealing with

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:28.760
is related to the average
kinetic energy of the particles.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.450
These particles are all
moving around, zooming around,

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:34.740
and they each would have
some kinetic energy.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.490
Remember, kinetic energy,
you calculate that

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:39.260
as MV squared over two.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:41.470
So, each of these particles
would have some mass

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:43.100
and some velocity,

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.770
but they could all have
different velocities for sure,

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:47.650
even if they're the same type of particle.

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:49.060
And if they're different
types of particles,

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:51.370
they can have different masses as well.

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:53.700
But, the average of these kinetic energies

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.490
across all of these particles,

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:58.880
that is proportional to temperature
when measured in Kelvin.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:01.951
And pressure, the pressure, remember,

00:01:01.951 --> 00:01:04.170
pressure is nothing but
force per unit area.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:08.200
And so, you can imagine this
surface of our container,

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.610
this could be some type of a cube

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:11.200
so I can draw it in 3-dimensions here,

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.240
so there's some area over here.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.110
And you have your particles,

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:17.720
let me do this in a different color,

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.430
these particles are
constantly bouncing off of it

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.850
and there's way more particles
then what I have drawn here,

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:25.350
so at any given moment,
you're having some particles

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:29.110
that are bouncing off of
this side of the container,

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:30.677
actually all sides of the container.

00:01:30.677 --> 00:01:33.070
And these are perfectly
elastic collisions,

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:34.990
they're preserving kinetic energy.

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:37.840
And so, they're applying
some force, collectively,

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:40.980
on this area, so the pressure is because

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:44.280
of these particle
collisions on the surface.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.790
Now, what I wanna do in this
video is take these ideas

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:50.320
that we conceptualize in
kinetic molecular theory

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:55.320
and to understand why the ideal
gas law, PV is equal to nRT,

00:01:56.870 --> 00:02:00.340
make sense when we
conceptualize the world here.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:04.113
Just a reminder, P is
pressure, V is volume,

00:02:04.113 --> 00:02:07.300
n is the number of moles of
whatever gas we're dealing with,

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:08.850
the amount of that gas,

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:11.060
and then T is the temperature in Kelvin,

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:12.930
and R is just the ideal gas constant,

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:14.720
that's just whatever
constant you're doing,

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.610
so that the units all work out together.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:20.300
So, let's first think about
how pressure relates to volume

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.670
if we were to old
everything else constant.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:27.500
Well, the idea gas law tells
us that pressure times volume

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.070
is going to be equal to this,
if we hold it at constant,

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:32.660
I can even just write
a K here for constant,

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:34.650
but that would also mean we could divide,

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:36.460
let's say both sides by V,

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:40.920
we can say that pressure is
equal to some constant over V.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.370
Another way to think about
it is is that pressure

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:46.860
is proportional to the inverse of volume.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.440
You could also write this,
if we divide both sides by P,

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.970
is that volume is proportional
to the inverse of pressure.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:53.960
Does that make sense

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.820
from a kinetic molecular
theory point of view?

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.620
Pause this video and think about it.

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:04.050
Well, imagine we have our
original cube right over here.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.770
And I had the same number of particles,

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:08.040
they have the same average kinetic energy,

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.290
but let's say I were
to increase the volume.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:13.610
So, if I were to make the volume go up,

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:15.580
so I were to some how expand this,

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:17.530
or maybe put the exact number,

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.820
the same particles with
the same temperature,

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:22.950
in a larger container,
then at any given moment,

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:25.070
you're just gonna have
fewer bounces of particles

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:26.910
off of the container.

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.520
Because they just have more
room to go in that volume,

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:31.100
and even the surface area of the container

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:32.810
is going to be high as well.

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:35.380
So, it makes sense that
if the volume goes up,

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:38.770
the pressure is going to go down.

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:40.370
And you can think about it the other way.

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:43.190
If you make this smaller,
that same number of particles

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:44.720
with the same average kinetic energy,

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:46.010
they're just gonna bump into the container

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:47.500
that much more often.

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:49.360
And that's going to increase the pressure,

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:53.720
so volume goes down, pressure goes up.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:55.760
And this relationship, that pressure

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.840
is inversely proportional
to volume, or vice versa,

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:00.400
if you hold everything else constant,

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.300
that's often known as Boyle's law.

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:06.350
Now, another relationship,
what if we were to hold volume

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:07.910
and the number of moles constant,

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:09.370
and we wanna think about the relationship

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:12.480
between pressure and temperature.

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:15.290
Well, this is constant, this
constant, and this is constant,

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:17.670
the ideal gas law would say that pressure

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:20.230
is going to be proportional to temperature

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:22.440
or that temperature's
proportional to pressure.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:23.820
Does that make sense?

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:25.950
Well, let's go back to
our original container.

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:27.870
If you were to increase the temperature,

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:30.580
that means that the average
kinetic energy is increased.

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:31.970
That means that these particles,

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:33.840
when they hit the side of the container,

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.910
they're going to hit
it with more velocity.

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:40.060
That means that you're going
to have, at any given moment,

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:41.730
you're gonna have more pressure

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:44.780
exerted on the side of the container.

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:46.080
And you could go the other way.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:47.500
Think about lowering the temperature,

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:49.650
so the kinetic energy goes really low,

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:52.790
then these particles are
just slowly drifting.

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:54.920
And the speed with which they are hitting

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:57.020
the side of the container
is going to go down

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:58.890
and so the pressure would go down.

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:00.120
So, it completely makes sense,

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:02.280
if temperature goes up, pressure goes up,

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:04.770
if temperature goes
down, pressure goes down,

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:09.110
and this is often known
as Gay-Lussac's law.

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:11.370
Now, another relationship, and
I'm really just going through

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:13.490
all of the combinations over here,

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:14.477
what if we were to hold pressure

00:05:14.477 --> 00:05:16.210
and the number of molecules constant?

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:17.430
So, we're really looking
at the relationship

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:19.350
between volume and temperature.

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:22.520
So, once again, if P, n,
and R is always constant,

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:25.100
if those are constant, the
ideal gas law would tell us

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:28.460
that the volume is proportional
to the temperature,

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:31.360
once again, holding
everything else constant.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:33.060
Well, to think about
that you can go through

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:35.110
that same thought experiment we just had.

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:37.690
If we increase the temperature,

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:39.640
if these things are moving around faster,

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:42.660
if you want to have the
same amount of force

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:46.010
per area on the container,
on the side of the container,

00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:49.100
you're going to have
to increase the volume.

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:51.240
So, this relationship, which
is completely consistent

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:52.840
with kinetic molecular theory,

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:55.610
is often known as Charles's law.

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:59.050
Now, another one is the
relationship between volume

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:01.070
and the number of moles.

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:02.940
If everything else is held constant,

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:05.080
the ideal gas law would
tell us that volume

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:08.730
is going to be proportional
to the number of moles

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:12.040
of our particle, or of our
gas that we are dealing with.

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:13.770
And that makes sense 'cause, once again,

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:15.040
you're holding everything else constant,

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:16.670
you want pressure to be constant,

00:06:16.670 --> 00:06:18.140
temperature to be constant.

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:21.660
If I were to double the
number of particles here,

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:24.040
but I don't wanna change the
pressure or the temperature,

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:26.110
makes sense that I would
have to double the volume.

00:06:26.110 --> 00:06:29.120
Likewise, if I wanted to
double the volume here

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:31.310
and I didn't wanna change the
pressure or the temperature,

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:33.590
I would have to put twice
as many particles in there,

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:36.270
so I still have sufficient
number of interactions

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:37.620
of bouncing of the particles

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:39.120
with the sides of the container,

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:41.690
so that I have sufficient pressure.

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:44.550
And this notion is called Avogadro's law.

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:45.890
Last but not least,

00:06:45.890 --> 00:06:48.013
let's say I have two identical containers.

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:50.740
I have two identical containers,

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:54.490
that's one there, that's one over here,

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:57.653
actually I'm gonna draw that
same container a third time.

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:03.670
And let's say over here, I have gas one

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:08.220
and in this case, it has
some pressure due to gas one,

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:10.150
we're gonna assume the
volume and the temperatures

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:12.350
are the same across all three of these.

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:14.820
And let's say we have gas two

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:17.580
and it is exerting pressure too,

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:20.280
if I were to take all of
the gas in both of them,

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:22.910
and put them both into
this third container,

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:26.040
so this third container is
gonna have all of the original

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:30.140
of gas one and all of
the original of gas two,

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:32.220
but we aren't changing the volume

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:34.020
and we aren't changing the temperature.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:36.910
In any given unit area on
the surface of the container,

00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:38.910
you're gonna get the
collisions from particle one,

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:43.150
which would give you P1 in
that force per unit area

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:46.040
and you're going to get the
collisions from particle two,

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:48.450
which would give you
that force per unit area.

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:51.900
So, it makes sense that the
partial pressures would add up

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:55.160
to be equal to the total
pressure in the container.

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:57.400
And this is known as Dalton's law.

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:00.380
But, the whole point of this
video is just appreciate

00:08:00.380 --> 00:08:01.840
that everything we've talked about

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:04.630
with the ideal gas law,
actually makes a lot of sense,

00:08:04.630 --> 00:08:06.520
I would argue it makes the most sense,

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:07.370
when you think about it

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:09.883
in terms of kinetic molecular theory.

